  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Utkarsh has a permutation PPP of {0,1,2,…,N−1}\\{0, 1, 2, \\dots, N-1\\}{0,1,2,…,N−1}.\n\nHe wants to find the number of pairs (L,R)(L, R)(L,R) (1≤L&lt;R≤N)(1 \\leq L \\lt R \\leq N)(1≤L&lt;R≤N) such that swapping the elements PLP_LPL​ and PRP_RPR​ does not change the MEX of any subarray.\n\nFormally, find the number of pairs (L,R)(L, R)(L,R) (1≤L&lt;R≤N)(1\\leq L \\lt R \\leq N)(1≤L&lt;R≤N) satisfying the following condition:\n\n\nLet P′P'P′ be the permutation defined as follows:\n\nPi′=PiP'_i = P_iPi′​=Pi​ for i∈{1,2,3,…,N}∖{L,R}i \\in \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, N\\} \\setminus \\{L, R\\}i∈{1,2,3,…,N}∖{L,R}\nPL′=PRP'_L = P_RPL′​=PR​\nPR′=PLP'_R = P_LPR′​=PL​\n\n\nmex({Pi,Pi+1,…,Pj})=mex({Pi′,Pi+1′,…,Pj′})mex(\\{P_i, P_{i+1}, \\ldots, P_j\\}) = mex(\\{P'_i, P'_{i+1}, \\ldots, P'_j\\})mex({Pi​,Pi+1​,…,Pj​})=mex({Pi′​,Pi+1′​,…,Pj′​}) for every 1≤i≤j≤N1 \\leq i \\leq j \\leq N1≤i≤j≤N\nFor example, let N=7N = 7N=7 and P=[2,5,1,0,3,6,4]P = [2, 5, 1, 0, 3, 6, 4]P=[2,5,1,0,3,6,4]. Then,\n\n\nSwapping the pair (1,3)(1, 3)(1,3), i.e swapping P1=2P_1 = 2P1​=2 and P3=1P_3 = 1P3​=1 would give us the permutation P′=[1,5,2,0,3,6,4]P' = [1, 5, 2, 0, 3, 6, 4]P′=[1,5,2,0,3,6,4]. This changes the mex of the subarray [3,4][3, 4][3,4], because mex({P3,P4})=mex({1,0})=2mex(\\{P_3, P_4\\}) = mex(\\{1, 0\\}) = 2mex({P3​,P4​})=mex({1,0})=2 but mex({P3′,P4′})=mex({2,0})=1mex(\\{P'_3, P'_4\\}) = mex(\\{2, 0\\}) = 1mex({P3′​,P4′​})=mex({2,0})=1, so (1,3)(1, 3)(1,3) is not a valid swap.\nSwapping the pair (4,7)(4, 7)(4,7) gives us the permutation P′=[2,5,1,4,3,6,0]P' = [2, 5, 1, 4, 3, 6, 0]P′=[2,5,1,4,3,6,0]. This changes the mex of the subarray [7,7][7, 7][7,7] — mex({P7})=mex({4})=0mex(\\{P_7\\}) = mex(\\{4\\}) = 0mex({P7​})=mex({4})=0 and mex({P7′})=mex({0})=1mex(\\{P'_7\\}) = mex(\\{0\\}) = 1mex({P7′​})=mex({0})=1. So, (4,7)(4, 7)(4,7) is also not a valid pair.\nHowever, it can be verified that the pair (2,6)(2, 6)(2,6) is valid — swapping P2P_2P2​ and P6P_6P6​ gives us the permutation P′=[2,6,1,0,3,5,4]P' = [2, 6, 1, 0, 3, 5, 4]P′=[2,6,1,0,3,5,4], and mex(P[i…j])=mex(P′[i…j])mex(P[i \\ldots j]) = mex(P'[i \\ldots j])mex(P[i…j])=mex(P′[i…j]) for every 1≤i≤j≤71 \\leq i \\leq j \\leq 71≤i≤j≤7.\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain an integer TTT, the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nDescription of each test case:\nThe first line contains a single integer NNN, denoting the size of the permutation PPP.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​, denoting the permutation PPP.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the number of pairs (L,R)(L,R)(L,R) such that swapping PLP_LPL​ and PRP_RPR​ does not change the MEX of any subarray.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\leq T \\leq 10^41≤T≤104",
      "2≤N≤1052 \\leq N \\leq 10^52≤N≤105",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105",
      "It is guaranteed that PPP is a permutation of {0,1,2,…,N−1}\\{0, 1, 2, \\dots, N-1\\}{0,1,2,…,N−1}."
